# Independent source reasoning, before alternatives

Authority: the single official inspect projection for modern 8a / alg8-c2-s1-n8.
All 15 written exercises, 102 through 116, are selected.
Definition blocks selected: 1, 2, 16, 25, 26. Blocks 1 and 2 define simultaneous truth and intersection; 16 names substitution generally without copying any example computation; 25 describes its applicability to linear systems; 26 distinguishes the special treatment of two quadratic equations. All worked-example paragraphs are excluded from definition authority. The inventory's fig-16 belongs to a worked example, not these exercises, and is not selected. There are no exercise figures.

All variables are real. Ordered pairs are (x,y) unless the word task explicitly assigns other roles.

## 102: substitution

a: 5(3y+5)+11y=-1 gives 26y=-26, hence (2,-1).
b: 7(-2y-1)-20y=146 gives -34y=153, hence (8,-9/2).
c: y=12x-16; 5x+3(12x-16)=75 gives 41x=123, hence (3,20).
d: x=(2-6y)/7; 6(2-6y)-35y=651 gives -71y=639, hence (8,-9).
e: x=(43-3y)/4; 5(43-3y)-28y=172 gives -43y=-43, hence (10,1).
f: x=(4+9y)/8; -3(4+9y)+32y=8 gives 5y=20, hence (5,4).
The six systems have independent coefficients and solutions; each can form one whole-result response, not a separate click for x and y. Selecting results does not demonstrate producing the substitution steps.

## 103: substitution, factorization, both branches

a: x=y+2 gives y^2-4y+3=0: (3,1),(5,3).
b: x=3y+2 gives 3y^2+8y-3=0=(3y-1)(y+3): (3,1/3),(-7,-3).
c: x(x-5y)=10 and x-5y=1 give x=10,y=9/5.
d: x=(y+3)/2 gives y^2+5y-6=0: (2,1),(-3/2,-6).
e: x=2(y+1)/3 gives 13y^2+8y-896=0, discriminant 216^2: (6,8),(-66/13,-112/13).
f: x=3y/2 gives y^2=16: (6,4),(-6,-4).
g: (2x-3y)(2x+3y)=15, so 2x-3y=3 and 2x+3y=5: (2,1/3).
h: x=2y+1 gives y^2+y-2=0: (3,1),(-3,-2).
i: y=(-1-3x)/2 gives x^2-8x+15=0: (3,-5),(5,-8).
Each response must preserve the complete set and the pairing of coordinates. Algebraic production is deferred.

## 104: all intersections and tangency

a: y=x-5, hence 2x(x-5)=0. Intersections (0,-5),(5,0); the line is a secant, not a tangent.
b: (x+y)^2=4 and (x-y)^2=36. Intersections (4,-2),(-2,4),(2,-4),(-4,2), all transverse.
c: u=x^2>=0 gives u(u-36)=0. Intersections (0,-10),(6,8),(-6,8). The circle and parabola share horizontal tangent y=-10 at (0,-10). Near x=0 the lower circle is -10+x^2/20+..., while the parabola is -10+x^2/2, so they touch there, not cross. At x=6 slopes are -3/4 and 6, at x=-6 they are 3/4 and -6: both other intersections cross.
A complete intersection/tangency statement permits mathematical recognition without a diagram. Plotting curves accurately, locating intersections from a graph, and graphical execution are not demonstrated and remain deferred. No ad hoc diagrams are required for these recognition tasks.

## 105: intersection of three solution sets

a: the first two equations force (5,-5), which gives x^2-y^2=0, not 40: empty set.
b: the first two equations force (1,0), which gives x^2+y^2=1, not 2: empty set.
Checking only two equations is insufficient.

## 106: reciprocal equations and nonzero domains

All require x!=0 and y!=0 before multiplying by xy.
a: x+y=10, xy=24: (4,6),(6,4).
b: x-y=6, xy=40: (10,4),(-4,-10), including the negative solution.
c: y=5-2x, x+y=(6/5)xy gives 12x^2-35x+25=0: (5/3,5/3),(5/4,5/2).
d: y=2x+3, y-x=xy/10 gives 2x^2-7x-30=0: (6,15),(-5/2,-2), including the negative solution.
All listed coordinates are nonzero and pass the original reciprocals; denominator exclusions and sign branches remain explicit.

## 107: numbers are not automatically positive

a: t^2-20t+96=0 gives the unordered numbers 8 and 12.
b: write x-y=6, xy=216. y^2+6y-216=0 gives ordered possibilities (18,12),(-12,-18). Both signed number pairs are allowed.

## 108: physical lengths and area

a: a,b>0, a+b=41, a^2+b^2=841. ab=420, so side lengths 20 m and 21 m.
b: legs a,b>0, a+b=47, a^2+b^2=1369. ab=420; area ab/2=210 cm^2.
Perimeter, hypotenuse/diagonal and half-product have distinct roles.

## 109: changes have assigned roles and feasible dimensions

a: new dimensions L,W satisfy L+W=46 and LW=240. Roots 40 and 6. Since W<=8, only L=40,W=6 is physically admissible, so length decreases 20 m and width decreases 2 m. Swapping the root assignment would increase the original width and is rejected.
b: let a be the side receiving +8 m, b the side receiving +2 m. a+b=40 and (a+8)(b+2)=3ab/2 imply a^2-52a+672=0. Both assignments (a,b)=(24,16),(28,12) work, respectively new dimensions (32,18),(36,14). The wording does not warrant choosing only one root; increments must not be swapped.

## 110: actual decimal digits

a: N+2s=96, Ns=952 yield N^2-96N+1904=0, N=68 or 28. For 68 the digit sum is 14, satisfying both conditions. For 28 the inferred s=34 disagrees with its actual digit sum 10. Only 68.
b: write N=10u+v, reverse=10v+u. Adding 27 gives reversal, so v-u=3. u^2+v^2=65 gives u=4,v=7; the other algebraic root u=-7 is not a digit. Only 47. "Same two digits" means reversal, not an unrestricted digit-sum condition.

## 111: meeting times, full-route arrival order, units

a: u=v_A>0,v=v_B>0, u+v=28 and 28/u-28/v=35/60=7/12. This gives u=12 or 112; 112 would force v=-84 and is inadmissible. A rider 12 km/h, B rider 16 km/h; full-route times 7/3 h and 7/4 h differ by 7/12 h.
b: p=v_M>0,q=v_N>0; p+q=45/(20/60)=135 and 45/q-45/p=9/60=3/20. q=60 or 675; latter forces negative p. M train 75 km/h, N train 60 km/h; full-route times 3/5 h and 3/4 h differ by 3/20 h.

## 112: rates, not addition of full-job times

a: individual full-tank times t,s>0. 1/t+1/s=1/12 and t/2+s/2=25 give t+s=50, ts=600: 20 and 30 min. Their half-tank times are 10 and 15 min, not the requested full-tank times.
b: fast full-job time t>0, slow t+24. 1/t+1/(t+24)=1/35 gives t^2-46t-840=0: t=60 or -14. Reject -14; fast 60 h, slow 84 h.

## 113: finite checks and general proof are different tasks

F(5,1)=25+15+1=41 and F(1,5)=1+15+25=41.
For arbitrary real a,b, F(b,a)=b^2+3ba+a^2=a^2+3ab+b^2=F(a,b), by commutativity. Thus every solution remains a solution after reversal. Testing the two named pairs alone does not prove the universal implication. A choice can recognize the full identity-based argument but cannot demonstrate independently writing the required proof; that production task is deferred.

## 114: exact radicals, including the mixed term

a: x^2=12, y^2=3, xy=sqrt(36)=6. Total 21, so not a solution.
b: x+y=6, xy=9-2=7. x^2+xy+y^2=(x+y)^2-xy=36-7=29, so a solution.

## 115: principal square roots and domains

a: x>=0,y>=0. Let t=sqrt(y)>=0. All solutions are (x,y)=((t+1)^2,t^2). Two distinct examples: (1,0),(4,1).
b: y>=0,x+y>=0. Let t=sqrt(y)>=0. All solutions are (x,y)=(2t+1,t^2). Two distinct examples: (1,0),(3,1).
Squaring sqrt(A)-sqrt(B) is not A-B. Negative radicands never become valid by algebraic squaring. Selecting candidate pairs does not demonstrate generating arbitrary solutions.

## 116: open quadrants are not coordinate axes

a: y=x^2+4>0. Open quadrants I and II, y-axis point (0,4), no x-axis point.
b: y=9-x^2 is positive for |x|<3, negative for |x|>3. All four open quadrants, x-axis points (-3,0),(3,0), y-axis point (0,9).
c: xy=6 needs matching signs: I and III; no axis point.
d: xy=-12 needs opposite signs: II and IV; no axis point.
One complete sign/axis table is a coherent comparison task; each cell is not a separate response.

## Capacity boundary

These are author derivations, not semantic approval. No answer key, historical choice, interaction, worked-example solution, answer pipeline output or parent review was used. Independent reviewer must solve from seed and then see only learner views first. All written computation production, graph drawing/reading execution in 104, proof writing in 113, and arbitrary solution generation in 115 remain deferred and will be named in draft coverage.
